  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: We're in for a couple more pleasant fall days before \nrainier conditions arrive over the weekend. In the meantime, high \npressure will dominate our weather, keeping the weather dry through \nthe rest of the work week. Dense valley fog will dissipate by mid \nmorning or so, giving way to another afternoon with ample sunshine \nand highs in the 60s. \n\nLow pressure off the Mid Atlantic coast will start to make its slow \npush northward tonight and Friday. While rain will remain well to \nour south, mid/high clouds will increase tonight and Friday. This \nwill help to keep temperatures a little warmer than the past couple \nof nights, limiting frost formation to just portions of the \nAdirondacks and Northeast Kingdom (where the growing season has \nended). Therefore, don't anticipate any additional Frost Advisories \ntonight, but subsequent shifts will need to watch cloud and \ntemperature trends closely. Friday will feature partly cloudy skies \nand highs in the 60s to near 70F. North winds could be a little on \nthe breezy side, particularly in the Champlain Valley due to \nfunneling.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Precipitation chances continue to increase for the \nweekend with this forecast package as deterministic and ensemble \nguidance continue to come into better agreement in bringing a \ndeveloping low up along the East Coast on Saturday. The system then \nlooks to meander around off the New England coast for a bit before \nfinally shunting off to the east. While model consensus is on this \noverall pattern, exactly how far north/west the low approaches and \nhow strong it becomes is still uncertain at this time. As noted by \nthe previous forecaster, there remains considerable spread in \npotential QPF amounts; the 25th percentile vs 75th percentile range \nis 0-0.44in at BTV; 0.04-0.82in at MPV, and 0.13-1.50in at VSF (for \n48-hr totals ending at 8 am Mon). Accordingly, PoPs have come up as \nwell, particularly in southeast VT where 6-hr PoPs top 70% Sunday \nmorning. There's still some uncertainty when/where bands of rain \nwill rotate back into our region, but expect there will be waves of \nshowers through the weekend. \n\nThis system will also bring gusty winds to the region this weekend. \nThe NAM, in particular, shows a strong jet wrapping around this \nsystem, with 70-80kt at 850mb over/just off the MA/RI shore. This \njet weakens some as it pushes inland toward our area, but guidance \nstill indicates 50+ kt 850mb jet spreading over eastern and \nsouthern VT during the day Saturday. The GFS' winds are weaker, \nwith 35-45 kt at 850mb, but either way, breezy conditions are \nlooking likely over the weekend, especially over the higher \nterrain and on Lake Champlain. Temperatures will be cool as \nwell; some places may struggle to get into the 60s during the \nday, especially in eastern/central VT. Anyone who has outdoor \nplans this weekend should stay tuned for updates and perhaps \nhave a backup plan in place should the forecast continue to \ntrend toward poor weather.",
